\section*{2. Equation Types}
For a second-order PDE $Au_{xx}+2Bu_{xy}+Cu_{yy}+\cdots=0$, the discriminant is $B^2-AC$: positive is hyperbolic, zero parabolic, and negative elliptic.

\subsection*{(i) $\phi_{xy}+\phi=25$}
Here $A=C=0$ and $2B=1$, so $B^2-AC=1/4>0$. Therefore
\[\boxed{\text{the equation is linear, second-order, and hyperbolic.}}\]

\subsection*{(ii) Continuity with $u=\phi_x$, $v=\phi_y$}
Substitution into $u_x+v_y=0$ gives
\[
\phi_{xx}+\phi_{yy}=0,
\]
the Laplace equation. Here $A=C=1,B=0$, so $B^2-AC=-1<0$ and
\[\boxed{\text{the potential equation is linear, second-order, and elliptic.}}\]

\subsection*{(iii) Boundary-layer momentum equation}
The equation
\[
u\,u_x+v\,u_y=\frac1{Re}u_{yy}
\]
contains a second derivative only in $y$. Hence $A=B=0$ and $C\ne0$, giving zero discriminant. Thus
\[\boxed{\text{the equation is nonlinear, second-order, quasilinear, and parabolic.}}\]
It behaves as a marching equation in the streamwise coordinate $x$ when the velocity $u$ remains positive.

